  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1135 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nLAST OF THE MOISTURE:\n\nDry air continues to filter into the region. Also, an inversion\nassociated with that dry air has put a cap on the instability. \nAlthough there may be enough moisture for clouds and light \nshowers mainly over the higher terrain this afternoon. The \nbetter chances are along and east of the Divide. \n\n\nHOT AND DRY:\n\nOver the next several days high pressure is expected to drift over \nthe Desert Southwest. This will bring warmer temperatures and a\ndrier airmass to the region. All of that results in record high\ntemperatures this weekend and into next week for many locations.\nAlso, localized critical fire weather is possible through that \nperiod. Winds speeds in the afternoon may reach 25 mph in spots \nwith minimum humidity in the teens and single digits. Afternoon \nshowers may still be possible along the Divide, but elsewhere \nstays dry through mid next week. On Monday, a trough tracks over\nthe northern tier of the country, which may suppress the high \npressure slightly. Beyond that temperatures still look well \nabove normal and the high shifts to our southeast. This may \nbring moisture back into the area that could support afternoon \nstorms once again.",
